Title: Judge's Crossword Puzzle No. 78
Author: S. M. Walker
Date: 1928-11-17
Byline: Submitted by S. M. Walker, Represa, Cal.
Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1928-11-17_95/page/n31/mode/1up
Puzzle-Number: 78
Solution-Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1928-11-24_95/page/n31/mode/1up


ITCH#STRAP#PICK
N#LABOR#NAKED#I
KNOW#LEMON#TEST
YET#M#MAN#C#ATE
#W#CABOT#HAM#A#
SER#CARRION#ALA
PLUG#R#I#NOOSES
A#MERRYMAKERS#P
DROMIO#O#I#BABE
EAR#MORNING#YEN
#I#HEM#INGOT#N#
USE#S#CAT#D#ICE
TELL#SALAD#ACHE
A#SILOS#CONGO#L
HEED#STATE#ENDS


A1. This is an irritation. ~ ITCH
A4. This is supposed to be effective in persuading youth to righteousness. ~ STRAP
A8. This is useful in digging up the dirt. ~ PICK
A11. This is despised by the working classes. ~ LABOR
A12. The way Adam got his start in the world. ~ NAKED
A13. To have information. ~ KNOW
A15. What name is aptly applied to a person of acid disposition? ~ LEMON
A17. The faster you travel the harder it is to pass this. ~ TEST
A19. Nevertheless. ~ YET
A21. The people of Tennessee know where this originated. ~ MAN
A23. After Adam did this he had to move. ~ ATE
A24. An early navigator. ~ CABOT
A26. This is a bad actor. ~ HAM
A27. An abbreviation for something Emerson found in stones. ~ SER
A29. What many an old buzzard is looking for. ~ CARRION
A30. A popular mammy state (Abbr.) ~ ALA
A32. The high-hat of the nineties. ~ PLUG
A34. These kind of neckties are very unpopular. ~ NOOSES
A36. What night-clubs are inhabited by before the checks come 'round. ~ MERRYMAKERS
A38. The name of the Mike and Ike of the Comedy of Errors. ~ DROMIO
A40. Someone who hits and runs—home. ~ BABE
A42. A listening-in device. ~ EAR
A43. This comes after an all-night party. ~ MORNING
A46. This means money in Tokio. ~ YEN
A47. Only a reckless gambler would bet that this could go any higher. ~ HEM
A48. Mass of cast metal. ~ INGOT
A49. What some people seem to think a guest-towel is made for. ~ USE
A51. A small domesticated quadruped. ~ CAT
A52. This is hard drink. ~ ICE
A54. What a discreet young man will never do. ~ TELL
A56. This is a cold dish. ~ SALAD
A58. What is a pain in the neck? ~ ACHE
A59. In what is fodder packed? (Plural.) ~ SILOS
A60. The name given a negro who comes from the section of a large river in central Africa. ~ CONGO
A61. What wise men do when they receive a warning. ~ HEED
A62. What is Washington? (The right answer will fit.) ~ STATE
A63. What is supposed to meet with a hoopsnake? ~ ENDS

D1. What word describes something very black. ~ INKY
D2. A coagulum. ~ CLOT
D3. This is opposed to "gee." ~ HAW
D4. Old Man Sunshine. ~ SOL
D5. What a Californian editor calls a quake. ~ TREMOR
D6. Soon. ~ ANON
D7. This takes the cake. ~ PAN
D8. Flappers love to do this. ~ PET
D9. This comes from good head-work. ~ IDEA
D10. You can reach great heights with this—but there's a string to it. ~ KITE
D14. A post from which winding-stairs radiate. ~ NEWEL
D16. What kind of troubles are little ones? ~ MATRIMONIAL
D18. This kind of thing is old stuff. ~ STALE
D20. A lot of the Scotch receive this label. ~ MAC
D22. This should never be tipped. ~ CANOE
D25. In what place do mortals come in contact with the spirit world. ~ BARROOM
D26. Many a silly goose is located by this. ~ HONKING
D27. This is good for a quick turnover. ~ SPADE
D28. This is apt to fly around in many circles. ~ RUMOR
D30. What could a handsome young chemist do for a gold-digger? ~ ASSAY
D31. Name a famous quaker. ~ ASPEN
D33. This is a sign that the lady is engaged—at least for the evening. ~ GEM
D35. This is a heavenly body (not Gilda Gray's). ~ ORB
D37. These are usually found in poetry. (You must spell it in the older and more etymologically correct manner.) ~ RIMES
D39. Name an uplifting verb. ~ RAISE
D41. What have the following in common: harnessmakers, second-rate ball-players and judges? ~ BENCH
D44. An impossible condition in which to keep a bank roll. ~ INTACT
D45. Who did the Kaiser announce as his pardner some years ago? ~ GOD
D49. In what state is a Jew considered a gentile? ~ UTAH
D50. A conjunction that means otherwise. ~ ELSE
D51. In what are bad actors often found? ~ CAST
D52. A sacred image. ~ ICON
D53. Crossword fish. (Plural) ~ EELS
D55. This goes over the top. ~ LID
D56. This is a sign of trouble. ~ SOS
D57. This is a little deer. ~ DOE
D58. This makes its mark. ~ AGE


